    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Free Officers' coup of 22–23 July 1952 ended the monarchy, and on 18 June 1953 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was declared a republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-naguib">Muhammad Naguib</a> as president and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> as prime minister; Nasser took the presidency in 1956.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> fought Israel in 1948, 1956, 1967 and 1973, lost the Sinai Peninsula and the Gaza Strip in the Six-Day War, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anwar-sadat">Anwar Sadat</a> made peace in 1979, regaining Sinai by 1982 at the cost of expulsion from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a>.</li><li>The revolution of 25 January 2011 toppled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosni-mubarak">Hosni Mubarak</a> after thirty years, but the military removed the elected president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohamed-morsi">Mohamed Morsi</a> on 3 July 2013, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdel-fattah-el-sisi">Abdel Fattah el-Sisi</a> has ruled since, winning a third six-year term in December 2023.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> has the oldest continuous parliamentary tradition in the Arab world: its first popular assembly met in 1866, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, opened in 1869, still earned the state a record $9.4 billion in 2023.</p></aside>
    </section>

